-- ================================================================
-- guardians.vocabulary — the trust lattice, and nothing that is not it.
--
-- `Text` is an ordinary sort with an ordinary type parameter. The only unusual
-- thing is WHAT the parameter ranges over — a trust level rather than an element
-- type — and because anthill type arguments unify, that one choice is the whole
-- information-flow mechanism.
--
-- What belongs here is what every other file builds on and none owns. Anything
-- with a subject of its own has left: mail to email.anthill, the triage
-- declarations to spec.anthill.
-- ================================================================

-- Two points is all this example needs; an n-point lattice would cost O(n^2)
-- coercions (docs/design/high-level-api.md D2).
enum guardians.TrustLevel
  entity Untrusted            -- came from outside; may be attacker-authored
  entity Trusted              -- the organisation vouched for it
end

-- THE PARAMETER IS NOT A WORKAROUND. It is the only way an inner label reaches a
-- signature: without it `Text` is one type and `Email.send(body: Text[Trusted])`
-- cannot be written at all.
--
-- The `= ?` IS one, AND THE DOORS BELOW HAVE MOSTLY CLOSED IT. `sort Trust = ?`
-- still admits anything as a written type — `Text[Int64]` in a signature loads
-- clean, measured — so `TrustLevel` remains what these arguments are MEANT to
-- inhabit rather than what the DECLARATION requires. What changed is that
-- nothing can PRODUCE one: the only two constructors are `untrusted` and
-- `trusted`, each pinning its own level, and the entity behind them is
-- `internal`. So the inhabited vocabulary is closed by construction even though
-- the declared one is open, and a `Text[Int64]` position is now uninhabitable
-- rather than merely unreachable. The residue T70A2 still names is the declared
-- side. WI-20260822-T70A2, proposal 062.
{< Text carrying its trust level in a type parameter. `Trust` is an ordinary
   type argument and unifies like any other, which is the whole
   information-flow mechanism: a sink written `Text[Trusted]` cannot be reached
   by a `Text[Untrusted]`, and no separate taint analysis is involved.

   The label is decided at the MINT, not merely read at the sink. A
   `Text[Trusted]` cannot be MADE without `Permission[Vouch]`, so the sink check
   is a consequence rather than the mechanism — which is what makes the label
   mean something rather than merely propagate. >}
enum guardians.Text
  import anthill.prelude.{String, Permission}
  import guardians.{Vouch, approved}
  import guardians.TrustLevel.{Untrusted, Trusted}
  sort Trust = ?
  internal entity text(raw: String)

  -- THE FREE DOOR. `Untrusted` is the restrictive label, so minting one asks no
  -- authority: a boundary and a deployment mint at will, and the default a
  -- program falls into is the safe one.
  operation untrusted(raw: String) -> Text[Untrusted] = text(raw: raw)

  -- THE VOUCHED DOOR, AND BOTH TIERS OF ONE POLICY SIT ON IT. `Permission[Vouch]`
  -- says WHO may vouch for content as the organisation's own; `approved` says WHAT
  -- the organisation has vouched for. They fail differently and catch different
  -- programs, which is the same pairing `Email.send` already draws between a row
  -- and a `requires` (§5.4) — moved to the MINT, because that is the one site where
  -- the content is a literal and the precondition can be discharged at all.
  operation trusted(raw: String) -> Text[Trusted]
    requires approved(raw)
    effects {Permission[Vouch]} = text(raw: raw)
end

namespace guardians
  -- WHAT THE ORGANISATION HAS VOUCHED FOR, and it is one of the two tiers on
  -- `Text.trusted`. `Permission[Vouch]` says WHO may vouch; this says WHAT was
  -- vouched for. They fail differently and catch different programs: the row
  -- catches an agent minting anything at all, this catches trusted code vouching
  -- for a line nobody approved. lib/observe.anthill closes what the model may say
  -- on the way in; this closes what the organisation may put its name to, and the
  -- two are the same instinct at opposite ends.
  --
  -- IT IS A RELATION, NOT AN ENUM, and the difference is deliberate: the closed
  -- set is a set of VALUES a deployment can extend, not a sort a deployment would
  -- have to recompile against.
  --
  -- IT IS OVER `String`, NOT OVER `Text`, AND IT SITS AT THE MINT (WI-20260829-MCKTE).
  -- It used to be `requires releasable(body)` on `Email.send`. That could only
  -- ever discharge when the body was written INLINE at the call — measured: a body
  -- merely LET-BOUND to the cleared literal already failed it, the same reading
  -- that refuses rejected/letbound_recipient.anthill. So it could not survive
  -- trusted text being HANDED to an agent rather than minted by it, which is what
  -- `Triage.run`'s `wording` parameter now does. At the mint the argument is
  -- always a literal, so the obligation is dischargeable by construction — and
  -- `Email.send` needs no precondition at all, a `Text[Trusted]` being unable to
  -- exist unless both tiers were satisfied where it was made.
  --
  -- IT LIVES BESIDE THE DOOR THAT CONSUMES IT, and it did not always. It was in
  -- lib/email.anthill while the obligation sat on `Email.send`; once the obligation
  -- moved to `Text.trusted` (WI-20260829-MCKTE), leaving it there made
  -- vocabulary.anthill import from email.anthill while email.anthill imports `Text`
  -- from here — a file cycle, and a content whitelist filed under a module that no
  -- longer reads it.
  --
  -- LIBRARY, NOT DEPLOYMENT, and the placement is still load-bearing:
  -- `the_organisations_identity_is_a_deployment_fact_and_the_default_is_closed`
  -- loads the library WITHOUT any deployment, and `summarize`'s own instruction is
  -- minted from lib/, so a set that lived only in a deployment would fail there on
  -- an unapproved line instead of on the missing authority it exists to measure.
  {< Which content the organisation has vouched for. A statement about CONTENT,
     proved from the KB where a `Text[Trusted]` is MINTED — not about provenance,
     which is the label the typer decides. A deployment extends the set by
     asserting rows; nothing else may be vouched for. >}
  rule approved(?raw)
  fact approved("routine compliance copy")
  -- The task's own wording, vouched for like any other content: `run_triage`
  -- (lib/spec.anthill) mints it and hands it to the agent, and the organisation
  -- puts its name to it here rather than in the code that uses it.
  fact approved("Summarize the following emails as a concise bullet-point list. Clearly mark messages as SPAM: or SUSPICIOUS: if applicable.")
end

-- The authority to VOUCH for content as the organisation's own. `Triage.run`'s
-- spec row never grants it, so no generated agent can mint a `Text[Trusted]` —
-- and the refusal does not care where the agent's string came from, which is
-- what makes the relabel unspellable rather than merely refused at a sink.
sort guardians.Vouch
end

-- NO TRANSITIONS BETWEEN LEVELS, AND NOW NO WAY TO FORGE ONE EITHER. The first
-- half was always the design: there is no `widen` and no `declassify`, and the
-- pair that once declared them was deleted for being unreachable theatre —
-- `declassify` could not be called at all, nothing minting its `Approval` token.
--
-- THE SECOND HALF IS WI-20260829-MCKTE, and it was a real hole for a long time.
-- `text(raw: u.raw)` re-labelled any `Text[Untrusted]` as `Text[Trusted]` in one
-- line and loaded clean: the lattice pinned a label AT A SINK
-- (rejected/leak.anthill) and said nothing about how a label was ACQUIRED. A
-- guarded door beside an open window.
--
-- WHAT CLOSED IT IS NOT A GUARD ON THE ARGUMENT, and the failed attempts are
-- worth recording because each looks right. Sealing `text` alone only moves the
-- door: 23 sites need a `Text[Trusted]`, so a public `String -> Text[Trusted]`
-- has to exist beside the seal, and it launders exactly as the raw constructor
-- did. Restricting that door to literal arguments is a syntactic side condition
-- — not compositional, and a second mechanism bolted beside the type system
-- rather than falling out of it. And no permission can INSPECT the difference,
-- because at run time the author's own string and the attacker's bytes are the
-- same value.
--
-- THE DISTINCTION IS NOT IN THE BYTES. Provenance is not derived from content;
-- it is ASSERTED. So asserting it is an AUTHORITY, and `Permission[Vouch]` on
-- the `trusted` door is that authority. `Triage.run`'s spec row never grants it,
-- so no generated agent can mint a `Text[Trusted]` at all — and the refusal
-- never looks at the argument, which is why every route dies from one cause:
-- `text(raw: u.raw)`, `text(raw: m.from.local)` and `text(raw: m.id.value)` are
-- all refused alike, and `Address`/`MessageId` needed no change despite holding
-- attacker-authored bytes of their own.

-- CAPABILITIES ARE THE SORTS YOU ACQUIRE, so no marker sort stands in for one.
-- `LiveLlm provides Llm` is the sub-capability edge for real, so `-Permission[Llm]`
-- forbids `Permission[X]` for every `X <: Llm` (rejected/frontier_checker.anthill).
--
-- THAT IS ENTAILMENT, NOT THE DECLARED CONTRAVARIANCE, and they run opposite ways.
-- `fact Contravariant(sort: Permission, param: T)` is subsumption; the closure runs
-- covariantly in the capability and needed its own kernel rule
-- (`typing::permission_entails`) — the one thing 064 predicted would come free and
-- did not.

-- A second project effect, so the row checks have a label to widen BY that is not
-- `Modify`. The two widenings travel different arms of one check — a named label
-- as a TYPE, a `Modify` target as a RESOURCE (§5.6) — so wide_row.anthill and
-- wide_row_modify.anthill each catch a program the other does not.
sort guardians.Filesystem
end

namespace guardians
  import anthill.prelude.{Effect}
  import guardians.{Filesystem}

  -- The project's one effect KIND. Capabilities are arguments of `Permission[…]`,
  -- never bare row labels; `Permission` itself the prelude registers.
  fact Effect[T = Filesystem]
end
